    "report": "Phone numbers with hyphens are now recognised, and the change is committed on `main`, the current branch. I did not push and did not start a server.\n\n**What changed**\n\nPhone numbers are recognised in two places, and both now accept a single hyphen as a separator:\n\n- **Messaging** (`src/Contexts/Messaging/Domain/Conversation/ContactDetails.ts`): numbers are held back as `[hidden]`.\n- **Reviews** (`src/Contexts/Reviews/Domain/StayReview/ReviewRules.ts`): numbers are refused under `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n\nThe new pattern in both is `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}`. ADR 0063 says a change to what counts as a phone number is made in each context that holds it, so I changed both. I found no other copy of the rule in the front end, schemas or conventions.\n\nI added spec cases for `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78` in each context. Existing date and short-number cases still pass, and `2026-12-01` is not matched because it has only eight digits.\n\n**Checks**\n\n- Unit tests: 155 suites, 1052 tests, all passing.\n- E2E tests for messaging, reviews and review rules: 126 suites, 703 tests, all passing.\n- Typecheck, lint, format check and `check:conventions`: all pass.\n\n**What was unclear**\n\n- **Dates in a row:** the rule says each digit follows the previous one after a single space, dot or hyphen. So two dates separated by a single space, such as `2026-12-01 2026-12-04`, form a 16-digit run and are now treated as a phone number. Separating them with a word, as the existing specs do, avoids this. I followed the rule as written and did not add an exception. Say if you want one.\n- **ADRs:** I did not edit ADR 0055 or 0063. They describe the rule in general terms and do not list the separators. A new ADR would be the place to record the hyphen decision if you want it on file.",
